 come back to this critique  article  what they are trying to assess is can we  consider the hallmarks of aging  as a paradigm for the field by  gerontology  and so as we learned earlier on about  the definition of paradigms  it's something that can be used as  communication in the field  so for example if i was going to tell  you about my research p53  i could kind of legitimize my  contribution to aging  by going p43 is involved in cellular  senescence and this is one of the  hallmarks of aging  so it helps to put the audience at ease  and give them some kind of familiarity  and this is because everyone in the  field knows what you're talking about  and so you can see how it snowballs in  size as more people join the field  legitimize their work and so on but the  interesting thing is that i'm actually a  cancer researcher which is convenient to  mention at this point  because the hallmarks of aging appear to  mimic the hallmarks of cancer  which was published before the hallmarks  of aging by hannah hannah weinberg in  2000  before getting updated in 2011. and i  know that this maybe sounds a bit like a  side step  but it's brought up in this critique  article because the whole muscle cancer  seems to serve as a much better paradigm  for cancer  than the homostasion does for aging so  what are their reasons to explain this  well i'm going to more or less quote  from their critique article  but in the hallmarks of cancer the  primary cause of cancer  is mutations of tumor suppressor genes  that actually include my favorite  protein p53  but other proto-oncogenes and it's these  mutations that give rise to six  other secondary causes and it suits six  other secondary causes that are  considered in the hallmarks diagram  and so it doesn't actually include  mutations as being a hallmark  because it's kind of assumed as a given  and so the formalization of the  hallmarks  is such that you have a primary  mechanism that then can cause a variety  of different secondary 